nt a Queen (since two Queens on board), while building the pot with your strong draw.\n\nBut you\'re allowed to choose any value ≥100. To balance aggression and chip preservation, **bet 1,500**.\n\nAlternatively, smaller bet like 800–1000, but 100 is too small — looks weak.\n\nBut since 100 is the minimum, and larger bets are allowed, **a bet of 1,500** is reasonable.\n\nBut let\'s consider: if you bet 1,500, opponent may fold worse hands, call with Queens or Kings, or chase with draws. Given you have 13 outs, even if called, you’re in good shape. And if they fold, you take down a decent pot.\n\nChecking risks letting them bluff or check-raise, but you want to **control the pot and apply pressure**.\n\nReason:  \nYou have a very strong draw (flush + gutshot, ~12 outs), and the opponent showed weakness by checking after pre-flop aggression. This is a classic semi-bluff opportunity. Betting 1,500 applies pressure, builds the pot for when you hit, and may win immediately. Folding or checking loses expected value.\n\nAnswer: 1500'}
